 }], "InlineItem",ExpressionUUID->"638af33d-9d98-864b-9bbb-e912918348a3"]
}], "ChatOutput",
 TaggingRules-><|
  "CellToStringData" -> 
   "/create_nb\n# OPF Rigorous Audit and Predictive Test Harness\n\nThis \
notebook turns the OPF audit into a **full testable Wolfram Language \
workflow** with:\n\n- symbolic verification\n- variational derivation checks\n\
- background classification\n- linearization consistency tests\n- modal / \
dispersion tests\n- residual validation\n- PDE eigenmode benchmarks\n- \
reduced state-space and stability tests\n- reproducible timing and precision \
audits\n- external tech-stack benchmark hooks for CUDA / PyTorch\n\nIt is \
organized so that:\n\n1. **OPF assumptions are explicit**\n2. **derived \
equations are automatically tested**\n3. **modern built-in Wolfram structures \
are used where valid**\n4. **predictive testing is separated into symbolic, \
numeric, and systems layers**\n5. **printed indications and exported \
artifacts are produced**\n\n---\n\n## 0. Notebook Goals\n\nThis harness \
answers four different questions:\n\n### A. Is the symbolic OPF derivation \
internally correct?\nUse:\n- \
[VariationalMethods`EulerEquations](paclet:ref/VariationalMethods/\
EulerEquations) if available\n- explicit Euler\[Dash]Lagrange construction\n- \
residual comparison\n- background solution classification\n\n### B. Is the \
linearization mathematically consistent?\nUse:\n- first-order perturbation \
expansion\n- coefficient extraction\n- Jacobian / Hessian cross-checks\n- \
dispersion determinant consistency\n\n### C. Does the model admit stable or \
measurable modes?\nUse:\n- symbolic modal reduction\n- \
[Eigensystem](paclet:ref/Eigensystem)\n- \
[NDEigensystem](paclet:ref/NDEigensystem)\n- generalized operator \
normalization checks\n\n### D. Can the implementation become predictive?\n\
Use:\n- reproducible parameter sweeps\n- residual-based acceptance tests\n- \
state-space realizations\n- external benchmark comparisons against PyTorch / \
CUDA solvers\n\n---\n\n## 1. Initialization\n\n```wl\nClearAll[\"Global`*\"];\
\nNeeds[\"VariationalMethods`\"];\n\n$HistoryLength = 0;\n$Assumptions = \n  \
Element[{\[Rho]0, \[Chi]0, r0, \[Gamma], \[CapitalGamma], \[Omega], kx, ky, \
kz, \[Lambda], \[Alpha], \[Beta], \[Mu], \[CurlyEpsilon], m}, Reals] && \n   \
\[Rho]0 > 0 && \[Chi]0 > 0 && \[Alpha] > 0 && \[Beta] > 0 && \[Mu] > 0;\n\n\
coords4 = {t, x, y, z};\nspatialCoords = {x, y, z};\nfields = {\[Rho], \
\[Chi], r};\nfieldVars = Through[fields[t, x, y, z]];\n\nsay[msg_] := \
Print[Style[msg, 14, Bold, Darker[Blue]]];\npass[msg_] := Print[Style[\"PASS: \
\" <> msg, Darker[Green]]];\nfail[msg_] := Print[Style[\"FAIL: \" <> msg, \
Red]];\nwarn[msg_] := Print[Style[\"WARN: \" <> msg, Darker[Orange]];\n\n\
withTiming[label_, expr_] := Module[{tm, res},\n  say[label];\n  {tm, res} = \
AbsoluteTiming[expr];\n  Print[Row[{\"Time: \", NumberForm[tm, {8, 4}], \" \
s\"}]];\n  res\n];\n\nreportAssociation[name_, assoc_] := (\n  say[name];\n  \
Print[Dataset[assoc]];\n);\n\ngradSq4[head_] := Total[D[head[t, x, y, z], \
#]^2 & /@ coords4];\nboxLike[head_] := Total[D[head[t, x, y, z], {#, 2}] & /@ \
coords4];\nspatialLap[head_] := Laplacian[head[t, x, y, z], spatialCoords];\n\
kSq = kx^2 + ky^2 + kz^2;\n```\n\n---\n\n## 2. OPF Layer Definitions\n\n```wl\
\nlayer0 = <|\n  \"Topology\" -> \"Sphere\",\n  \"BoundaryDuality\" -> {0, \
Infinity},\n  \"TemporalMode\" -> \"Timeless\",\n  \"Substrate\" -> \
\"EmptySpacetime\"\n|>;\n\nlagrangianAlgebraic = \n  \[Rho][t, x, y, z] Log[\
\[Chi][t, x, y, z]] + \[Chi][t, x, y, z] r[t, x, y, z]^2;\n\n\
lagrangianRegularized = \n  lagrangianAlgebraic + \n   \[Alpha]/2 gradSq4[\
\[Rho]] + \n   \[Beta]/2 gradSq4[\[Chi]] + \n   \[Mu]/2 gradSq4[r];\n\n\
maintenanceEquation = D[\[Phi][t], t] + \[CapitalGamma] \[Phi][t] == 0;\n\
measurementFunctional[\[Psi]_, basis_, region_] := \n  Integrate[\[Psi] \
basis, Evaluate@Sequence @@ region, GenerateConditions -> True];\n\n\
say[\"Initialized OPF layers and regularized Lagrangian.\"];\n\
Print[lagrangianRegularized // TraditionalForm];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 3. \
Algebraic Stationarity Tests\n\n```wl\nalgEquations = Thread[D[\[Rho]s Log[\
\[Chi]s] + \[Chi]s rs^2, {{\[Rho]s, \[Chi]s, rs}}] == 0];\nalgSolve = \
FullSimplify[Solve[algEquations, {\[Rho]s, \[Chi]s, rs}, Reals]];\nalgReduce \
= FullSimplify[Reduce[algEquations, {\[Rho]s, \[Chi]s, rs}, Reals]];\n\n\
say[\"Algebraic stationarity equations:\"];\nPrint[algEquations // \
TableForm];\n\nsay[\"Exact stationary solutions:\"];\nPrint[algSolve // \
InputForm];\n\nsay[\"Reduce form of stationary constraints:\"];\n\
Print[algReduce // InputForm];\n\nstationaryBackground = {\[Rho]0 -> 0, \
\[Chi]0 -> 1, r0 -> 0};\n\nIf[algSolve =!= {}, pass[\"Algebraic stationary \
set found.\"], fail[\"No algebraic stationary set found.\"]];\n```\n\n**Test \
area in your tech stack:**\n- symbolic algebra layer\n- rule engine \
correctness\n- equation solving layer\n- assumption propagation\n\n---\n\n## \
4. Euler\[Dash]Lagrange Derivation: Explicit and Built-In Cross-Check\n\n\
```wl\neulerExplicit = Association[\n  \"\[Rho]\" -> FullSimplify[\n    \
D[lagrangianRegularized, \[Rho][t, x, y, z]] - \n     \
Total[D[D[lagrangianRegularized, D[\[Rho][t, x, y, z], #]], #] & /@ coords4]\n\
  ],\n  \"\[Chi]\" -> FullSimplify[\n    D[lagrangianRegularized, \[Chi][t, \
x, y, z]] - \n     Total[D[D[lagrangianRegularized, D[\[Chi][t, x, y, z], \
#]], #] & /@ coords4]\n  ],\n  \"r\" -> FullSimplify[\n    \
D[lagrangianRegularized, r[t, x, y, z]] - \n     \
Total[D[D[lagrangianRegularized, D[r[t, x, y, z], #]], #] & /@ coords4]\n  ]\n\
];\n\neulerBuiltIn = AssociationThread[\n  {\"\[Rho]\", \"\[Chi]\", \"r\"},\n \
 FullSimplify /@ EulerEquations[\n    lagrangianRegularized,\n    fieldVars,\n\
    coords4\n  ]\n];\n\neulerComparison = AssociationMap[\n  \
FullSimplify[eulerExplicit[#] == SubtractSides[eulerBuiltIn[#]]] &,\n  \
Keys[eulerExplicit]\n];\n\neulerSystem = Thread[Values[eulerExplicit] == 0];\n\
eulerLHS = Values[eulerExplicit];\n\nsay[\"Euler\[Dash]Lagrange explicit \
forms:\"];\nPrint[eulerExplicit // Dataset];\n\nsay[\"Euler\[Dash]Lagrange \
cross-checks:\"];\nPrint[eulerComparison // Dataset];\n\nIf[And @@ \
Values[eulerComparison],\n  pass[\"Explicit Euler derivation matches built-in \
EulerEquations structure.\"],\n  fail[\"Mismatch between explicit and \
built-in variational derivation.\"]\n];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech \
stack:**\n- symbolic variational calculus\n- regression checks on Lagrangian \
parsing\n- derivative operator consistency\n\n---\n\n## 5. Inactive Operator \
Preservation Audit\n\n```wl\ninactiveEulerSystem = Inactivate[eulerSystem, D \
| Laplacian | Div | Grad];\nactivatedEulerSystem = \
Activate[inactiveEulerSystem];\n\ninactiveAudit = \
FullSimplify[activatedEulerSystem == eulerSystem];\nPrint[\"Inactive/Activate \
audit: \", inactiveAudit];\nIf[TrueQ[inactiveAudit], pass[\"Inactive operator \
preservation verified.\"], fail[\"Inactive activation changed equations.\"]];\
\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- symbolic operator serialization\
\n- notebook export/import integrity\n- PDE operator transformation layer\n\n\
---\n\n## 6. Hidden Constraint Solve and Structural Simplification\n\n```wl\n\
hiddenConstraints = FullSimplify[\n  Solve[\n   eulerSystem /. {\n      \
Derivative[2, 0, 0, 0][f_][t, x, y, z] :> 0,\n      Derivative[0, 2, 0, \
0][f_][t, x, y, z] :> 0,\n      Derivative[0, 0, 2, 0][f_][t, x, y, z] :> 0,\n\
      Derivative[0, 0, 0, 2][f_][t, x, y, z] :> 0\n    },\n   fieldVars,\n   \
Reals,\n   MaxExtraConditions -> All\n  ]\n];\n\nsay[\"Hidden constraints \
under second-derivative suppression:\"];\nPrint[hiddenConstraints // \
InputForm];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- reduced model \
discovery\n- symbolic elimination engine\n- structural identifiability layer\n\
\n---\n\n## 7. Symbol Audit and Missing-Variable Report\n\n```wl\n\
actionSymbols = DeleteDuplicates @ Cases[\n   lagrangianRegularized,\n   \
s_Symbol /; Context[s] === \"Global`\",\n   Infinity\n];\n\nactionHeads = \
DeleteDuplicates @ Cases[\n   lagrangianRegularized,\n   h_Symbol[___] :> h,\n\
   Infinity\n];\n\nstandardModelLikeConstants = {c, h, \[HBar], G, kB, E, \
Subscript[\[Epsilon], 0], Subscript[\[Mu], 0]};\nopfSymbolsAudit = \
Complement[Join[actionSymbols, actionHeads], Join[coords4, {Log}]];\n\
missingVsStandardModel = Complement[opfSymbolsAudit, \
standardModelLikeConstants];\n\nreportAssociation[\n  \"Symbol audit\",\n  <|\
\n    \"AllGlobalSymbolsAndHeads\" -> opfSymbolsAudit,\n    \
\"MissingVsSMConstants\" -> missingVsStandardModel\n  |>\n];\n```\n\n**Test \
area in your tech stack:**\n- symbol registry\n- parser leaks\n- hidden \
dependency tracking\n\n---\n\n## 8. Background Classification with Reduce\n\n\
```wl\nbackgroundEquations = FullSimplify[eulerLHS /. {\n    \[Rho][t, x, y, \
z] -> \[Rho]0,\n    \[Chi][t, x, y, z] -> \[Chi]0,\n    r[t, x, y, z] -> r0\n\
}];\n\nbackgroundClassification = FullSimplify[\n  \
Reduce[Thread[backgroundEquations == 0], {\[Rho]0, \[Chi]0, r0}, Reals]\n];\n\
\nsay[\"Background classification:\"];\nPrint[backgroundClassification // \
InputForm];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- equilibrium finder\n\
- assumption-sensitive symbolic reduction\n\n---\n\n## 9. Linearization About \
the Background\n\n```wl\nlinRules = {\n  \[Rho] -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, \
\[Rho]0 + \[CurlyEpsilon] u[tt, xx, yy, zz]],\n  \[Chi] -> Function[{tt, xx, \
yy, zz}, \[Chi]0 + \[CurlyEpsilon] v[tt, xx, yy, zz]],\n  r -> Function[{tt, \
xx, yy, zz}, r0 + \[CurlyEpsilon] w[tt, xx, yy, zz]]\n};\n\nseries0 = \
Map[Normal[Series[#, {\[CurlyEpsilon], 0, 0}]] &, eulerLHS /. linRules];\n\
series1 = Map[Normal[Series[#, {\[CurlyEpsilon], 0, 1}]] &, eulerLHS /. \
linRules];\nbackgroundConditions = FullSimplify[series0];\nlinearizedSystem = \
FullSimplify[Coefficient[#, \[CurlyEpsilon]] & /@ series1];\n\n\
say[\"Background conditions from zeroth-order expansion:\"];\n\
Print[backgroundConditions // TableForm];\n\nsay[\"Linearized equations:\"];\n\
Print[linearizedSystem // TableForm];\n\nIf[backgroundConditions /. \
stationaryBackground === ConstantArray[0, Length[backgroundConditions]],\n  \
pass[\"Stationary background satisfies zeroth-order equations.\"],\n  \
warn[\"Chosen stationary background does not annihilate all zeroth-order \
terms exactly under current assumptions.\"]\n];\n```\n\n**Test area in your \
tech stack:**\n- perturbation engine\n- symbolic series expansion\n- \
regression on operator substitution\n\n---\n\n## 10. Hessian and \
Mass/Stiffness Cross-Checks\n\nThis extracts the local algebraic Hessian of \
the potential-like term and compares it to the zero-wave-number linear \
structure.\n\n```wl\npotentialPart = \[Rho]s Log[\[Chi]s] + \[Chi]s rs^2;\n\
potentialHessian = FullSimplify[D[potentialPart, {{\[Rho]s, \[Chi]s, rs}, \
2}]];\npotentialHessianAtStationary = FullSimplify[potentialHessian /. \
{\[Rho]s -> 0, \[Chi]s -> 1, rs -> 0}];\n\nsay[\"Potential Hessian at \
stationary background:\"];\nPrint[MatrixForm[potentialHessianAtStationary]];\n\
```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- local stability classification\n- \
tangent-linear model verification\n\n---\n\n## 11. Plane-Wave Modal Reduction\
\n\n```wl\nplaneRules = {\n  u -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, u0 Exp[I (kx xx \
+ ky yy + kz zz - \[Omega] tt)]],\n  v -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, v0 Exp[I \
(kx xx + ky yy + kz zz - \[Omega] tt)]],\n  w -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, \
w0 Exp[I (kx xx + ky yy + kz zz - \[Omega] tt)]]\n};\n\nmodalAlgebraic = \
FullSimplify[\n  (linearizedSystem /. stationaryBackground /. planeRules) /\n \
  Exp[I (kx x + ky y + kz z - \[Omega] t)]\n];\n\nstiffnessMatrix = \
Simplify[Coefficient[#, {u0, v0, w0}] & /@ modalAlgebraic];\ndispersionDet = \
FullSimplify[Det[stiffnessMatrix]];\neigenData = \
FullSimplify[Eigensystem[stiffnessMatrix]];\nmodalFrequencies = \
FullSimplify[Solve[dispersionDet == 0, \[Omega]]];\nstableFrequencyCondition \
= FullSimplify[Reduce[dispersionDet == 0 && Element[\[Omega], Complexes], \
\[Omega]]];\n\nsay[\"Modal stiffness matrix:\"];\n\
Print[MatrixForm[stiffnessMatrix]];\n\nsay[\"Dispersion determinant:\"];\n\
Print[dispersionDet // TraditionalForm];\n\nsay[\"Modal frequencies:\"];\n\
Print[modalFrequencies // InputForm];\n\nsay[\"Stable frequency \
condition:\"];\nPrint[stableFrequencyCondition // InputForm];\n```\n\n**Test \
area in your tech stack:**\n- symbolic spectral analysis\n- \
dispersion-relation testing\n- mode classification\n- wave solver \
verification\n\n---\n\n## 12. Stable-Mode Decision Functions\n\n```wl\n\
stableImaginaryModes[expr_] := Solve[\n  expr == 0 && Re[\[Omega]] == 0 && \
Im[\[Omega]] > 0,\n  \[Omega],\n  Complexes\n];\n\npurelyOscillatoryModes = \
FullSimplify[stableImaginaryModes[dispersionDet]];\n\nsay[\"Purely imaginary \
positive-frequency modes:\"];\nPrint[purelyOscillatoryModes // InputForm];\n\
```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- spectral filtering\n- \
OPF-specific stable-mode criterion\n\n---\n\n## 13. Residual Validation \
Harness\n\n```wl\nresidualVector[\[Rho]cand_, \[Chi]cand_, rcand_] := \
FullSimplify[\n  eulerLHS /. {\n    \[Rho] -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, \
\[Rho]cand],\n    \[Chi] -> Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, \[Chi]cand],\n    r -> \
Function[{tt, xx, yy, zz}, rcand]\n  }\n];\n\ntrivialResidualCheck = \
residualVector[0, 1, 0];\n\nsay[\"Residual at stationary background:\"];\n\
Print[trivialResidualCheck // TableForm];\n\nIf[trivialResidualCheck === {0, \
0, 0},\n  pass[\"Residual validation passed for stationary background.\"],\n  \
warn[\"Residual is not identically zero; inspect assumptions or branch \
conditions.\"]\n];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- acceptance \
testing\n- candidate solution validator\n- model auditing pipeline\n\n---\n\n\
## 14. Exact Persistence / Maintenance Law\n\n```wl\nmaintenanceSolution = \
DSolveValue[{maintenanceEquation}, \[Phi][t], t];\n\nsay[\"Exact maintenance \
law solution:\"];\nPrint[maintenanceSolution // TraditionalForm];\n```\n\n\
**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- exact ODE solver\n- persistence-law \
fitting\n\n---\n\n## 15. Measurement Kernel Tests\n\n```wl\n\[Psi]1[x1_] := \
Exp[-x1^2];\nbasis1[n_][x1_] := HermiteH[n, x1] Exp[-x1^2/2];\n\n\
measurementPrototypes = FullSimplify @ Table[\n   Integrate[\n    \[Psi]1[x1] \
basis1[n][x1],\n    {x1, -Infinity, Infinity},\n    GenerateConditions -> \
True\n   ],\n   {n, 0, 6}\n];\n\nprojectionCoefficient[n_, \[Psi]_] := \
Integrate[\n  \[Psi][x1] basis1[n][x1],\n  {x1, -Infinity, Infinity},\n  \
GenerateConditions -> True\n];\n\nsay[\"Measurement prototypes:\"];\n\
Print[measurementPrototypes // TableForm];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech \
stack:**\n- measurement projection layer\n- orthogonal basis expansion engine\
\n- observation-model verification\n\n---\n\n## 16. PDE Benchmark: Numerical \
Eigenmode Test\n\nThis is not the OPF PDE itself; it is a **solver sanity \
benchmark** using built-in eigenmode infrastructure. If this fails, your PDE \
numerics stack is suspect.\n\n```wl\npdeBench = withTiming[\n  \"Running \
numerical PDE eigenmode sanity test\",\n  Module[{vals, funs},\n    {vals, \
funs} = NDEigensystem[\n      {\n        -Laplacian[u[x, y], {x, y}],\n       \
 DirichletCondition[u[x, y] == 0, True]\n      },\n      u[x, y],\n      {x, \
y} \[Element] Disk[],\n      4\n    ];\n    <|\n      \"Eigenvalues\" -> \
vals,\n      \"L2NormsApprox\" -> (NIntegrate[#1[x, y]^2, {x, y} \[Element] \
Disk[]] & /@ funs)\n    |>\n  ]\n];\n\nreportAssociation[\"PDE eigenmode \
solver sanity benchmark\", pdeBench];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech \
stack:**\n- FEM stack\n- eigenmode solver\n- interpolation normalization\n- \
CPU numeric baseline\n\n---\n\n## 17. PDE Benchmark: \
Schr\[ODoubleDot]dinger-Type Spectral Test\n\n```wl\nhVal = 1/10;\nV[x_] := \
UnitStep[x] (2 x^2) + UnitStep[-x] (x^2/2);\nschOp = SchrodingerPDEComponent[\
\n  {\[CapitalPsi][x], {x}},\n  <|\n    \"SchrodingerPotential\" -> V[x],\n   \
 \"ReducedPlanckConstant\" -> hVal,\n    \"Mass\" -> 1/2\n  |>\n];\n\n\
schBench = withTiming[\n  \"Running Schr\[ODoubleDot]dinger spectral \
benchmark\",\n  Module[{vals, funs},\n    {vals, funs} = NDEigensystem[\n     \
 schOp,\n      \[CapitalPsi][x],\n      {x, -3, 3},\n      6,\n      Method \
-> {\"PDEDiscretization\" -> {\"FiniteElement\", {\"MeshOptions\" -> \
{MaxCellMeasure -> 0.05}}}}\n    ];\n    <|\n      \"Eigenvalues\" -> vals,\n \
     \"PotentialAt0\" -> V[0],\n      \"SampleNorms\" -> (NIntegrate[#1[x]^2, \
{x, -3, 3}] & /@ funs)\n    |>\n  ]\n];\n\nreportAssociation[\"Schr\
\[ODoubleDot]dinger solver benchmark\", schBench];\n```\n\n**Test area in \
your tech stack:**\n- one-dimensional spectral solver\n- mesh refinement \
sensitivity\n- operator construction correctness\n\n---\n\n## 18. State-Space \
Realization and Stability Tests\n\n```wl\nssmTemplate = StateSpaceModel[\n  {\
\n    x1'[t] == x2[t],\n    x2'[t] == -a x1[t] - b x2[t] + c uIn[t]\n  },\n  \
{x1[t], x2[t]},\n  {uIn[t]},\n  {x1[t]},\n  t\n];\n\ncontrollabilityCheck = \
ControllableModelQ[ssmTemplate];\nstabilityPoles = \
SystemsModelPoles[ssmTemplate];\n\nsay[\"State-space controllability:\"];\n\
Print[controllabilityCheck];\n\nsay[\"State-space poles:\"];\n\
Print[stabilityPoles // InputForm];\n```\n\n**Test area in your tech stack:**\
\n- control layer\n- reduced-order modeling\n- systems identification \
interface\n\n---\n\n## 19. Precision Audit\n\nYour OPF instructions request \
effectively unbounded precision. Built-in PDE solvers do not support literal \
infinite working precision in finite element workflows. The rigorous \
practical method is:\n\n1. use exact symbolic derivation where possible\n2. \
use arbitrary precision numerics where supported\n3. refine meshes / \
truncations\n4. perform convergence and residual testing\n5. reject results \
that are not stable under refinement\n\nThis section implements a practical \
convergence audit.\n\n```wl\nClearAll[precisionSweepResidual];\n\
precisionSweepResidual[prec_] := Module[{val},\n  val = N[dispersionDet /. {\
\[Alpha] -> 1, \[Beta] -> 1, \[Mu] -> 1, kx -> 1, ky -> 0, kz -> 0, \[Omega] \
-> I}, prec];\n  <|\"Precision\" -> prec, \"AbsValue\" -> Abs[val]|>\n];\n\n\
precisionAudit = precisionSweepResidual /@ {30, 50, 80, 120};\n\
reportAssociation[\"Precision audit\", \
AssociationThread[Range[Length[precisionAudit]], precisionAudit]];\n```\n\n\
**Test area in your tech stack:**\n- arbitrary precision arithmetic\n- \
convergence-control layer\n- residual tolerance policy\n\n---\n\n## 20. \
Predictive Testing Methodology\n\nA predictive model is not validated by \
elegance alone. Use this hierarchy:\n\n### Tier 1 \[LongDash] Symbolic \
correctness\n- Euler\[Dash]Lagrange agreement\n- residual identity checks\n- \
background classification\n- modal determinant consistency\n\n### Tier 2 \
\[LongDash] Numerical correctness\n- mesh refinement convergence\n- precision \
convergence\n- normalization audits\n- independent solver comparison\n\n### \
Tier 3 \[LongDash] Structural correctness\n- observability / controllability \
of reduced forms\n- stability under perturbation\n- parameter sensitivity\n- \
identification of singular / rigid modes\n\n### Tier 4 \[LongDash] Predictive \
correctness\n- fit on one dataset\n- predict on held-out datasets\n- compare \
forecast residuals\n- reject models that only interpolate\n\n### Tier 5 \
\[LongDash] Deployment correctness\n- benchmark on target hardware\n- CPU vs \
GPU reproducibility\n- deterministic seed control\n- pipeline logging and \
artifact export\n\n---\n\n## 21. External Tech-Stack Test Areas\n\nThese are \
the most relevant places to test outside Wolfram Language.\n\n### A. Python / \
SymPy layer\nUse for cross-checking:\n- algebraic stationary equations\n- \
Hessians / Jacobians\n- simplified dispersion determinants\n\n### B. PyTorch \
layer\nUse for:\n- autodiff verification of reduced losses / action densities\
\n- batched parameter scans\n- operator-learning surrogates\n- \
Jacobian-vector products\n\n### C. CUDA layer\nUse for:\n- batched residual \
evaluation over large grids\n- modal determinant sweeps\n- Monte Carlo \
parameter stress tests\n- accelerated surrogate inference\n\n### D. JAX layer \
if available\nUse for:\n- differentiable PDE residuals\n- compiled \
high-throughput gradient checks\n- sensitivity analysis\n\n### E. FEM / PDE \
external solvers\nUse when needed for cross-validation:\n- FEniCS / Firedrake \
for PDE cross-checks\n- PETSc/SLEPc for large eigensystems\n\n---\n\n## 22. \
PyTorch / CUDA Benchmark Hooks\n\nThe Wolfram side should export clean \
tensors / arrays for external runs.\n\n```wl\nbenchmarkExport = <|\n  \
\"StationaryBackground\" -> stationaryBackground,\n  \"StiffnessMatrix\" -> \
stiffnessMatrix,\n  \"DispersionDeterminant\" -> dispersionDet,\n  \
\"LinearizedSystem\" -> linearizedSystem,\n  \"ParameterPoint\" -> <|\"\
\[Alpha]\" -> 1., \"\[Beta]\" -> 1., \"\[Mu]\" -> 1., \"kx\" -> 1., \"ky\" -> \
0., \"kz\" -> 0.|>\n|>;\n\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"opf-benchmark-export.wl\"}], benchmarkExport];\n\
Export[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"opf-benchmark-export.json\"}], \
Normal[benchmarkExport], \"JSON\"];\n\nsay[\"Exported benchmark payload for \
external Python/CUDA stack.\"];\n```\n\n### Recommended external benchmarks\n\
\n#### PyTorch benchmark targets\n- evaluate dispersion determinant on \
$$10^5$$ to $$10^7$$ parameter points\n- compare CPU and GPU timings\n- \
compare float64 against arbitrary-precision reference samples from Wolfram \
Language\n- compute gradient agreement for local linearization terms\n\n#### \
CUDA benchmark targets\n- custom kernel for residual field evaluation on \
large 4D grids\n- batched complex determinant evaluation for modal scans\n- \
reduction kernels for max residual and norm summaries\n\n#### Acceptance \
criteria\n- symbolic reference agreement on randomly sampled test points\n- \
relative error below chosen tolerance, e.g. $$10^{-10}$$ for float64 tasks\n- \
no sign or branch inconsistencies in modal roots\n\n---\n\n## 23. Suggested \
Python Benchmark Skeleton\n\n```python\nimport json\nimport time\nimport \
torch\n\nwith open(\"opf-benchmark-export.json\", \"r\") as f:\n    payload = \
json.load(f)\n\n# Replace this with a hand-transcribed or generated \
determinant function.\ndef dispersion_det(alpha, beta, mu, kx, ky, kz, \
omega):\n    ksq = kx*kx + ky*ky + kz*kz\n    # placeholder: replace with \
exported symbolic expression\n    return alpha*(omega**2 + ksq) + \
beta*(omega**2 + ksq) + mu*(omega**2 + ksq)\n\n\ndef bench(device=\"cuda\", \
n=1_000_000):\n    torch.set_default_dtype(torch.float64)\n    alpha = \
torch.ones(n, device=device)\n    beta = torch.ones(n, device=device)\n    mu \
= torch.ones(n, device=device)\n    kx = torch.randn(n, device=device)\n    \
ky = torch.randn(n, device=device)\n    kz = torch.randn(n, device=device)\n  \
  omega = 1j * torch.abs(torch.randn(n, device=device, \
dtype=torch.float64)).to(torch.complex128)\n\n    torch.cuda.synchronize() if \
device == \"cuda\" else None\n    t0 = time.time()\n    val = \
dispersion_det(alpha, beta, mu, kx, ky, kz, omega)\n    \
torch.cuda.synchronize() if device == \"cuda\" else None\n    t1 = \
time.time()\n    print({\n        \"device\": device,\n        \"n\": n,\n    \
    \"elapsed_sec\": t1 - t0,\n        \"sample_abs_mean\": \
torch.mean(torch.abs(val)).item()\n    })\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n   \
 bench(\"cpu\", 100000)\n    if torch.cuda.is_available():\n        \
bench(\"cuda\", 1000000)\n```\n\n---\n\n## 24. True Rigorous Testing Strategy\
\n\nThe most rigorous method is not a single solver. It is a **stack of \
mutually constraining tests**:\n\n### Symbolic truth layer\n- derive \
equations exactly\n- verify same equations in two ways\n- classify equilibria \
with [Reduce](paclet:ref/Reduce)\n- inspect residual identities\n\n### \
Numerical truth layer\n- use [NDEigensystem](paclet:ref/NDEigensystem) and \
[NDEigenvalues](paclet:ref/NDEigenvalues) for benchmark PDEs\n- perform \
refinement sweeps\n- monitor normalization and residuals\n- compare to \
alternative solvers where possible\n\n### Systems truth layer\n- reduce to \
[StateSpaceModel](paclet:ref/StateSpaceModel)\n- inspect poles, \
controllability, observability\n- test stability margins\n\n### Hardware \
truth layer\n- compare CPU symbolic references against GPU batched evaluation\
\n- track deterministic reproducibility\n- ensure no dtype downgrades\n\n### \
Predictive truth layer\n- hold out data\n- predict unseen conditions\n- \
reject unstable parameterizations\n- report calibration and forecast error \
separately\n\n---\n\n## 25. Final Summary Object\n\n```wl\nfinalSummary = <|\n\
  \"Layer0\" -> layer0,\n  \"RegularizedLagrangian\" -> \
lagrangianRegularized,\n  \"AlgebraicEquations\" -> algEquations,\n  \
\"AlgebraicSolutions\" -> algSolve,\n  \"BackgroundClassification\" -> \
backgroundClassification,\n  \"EulerExplicit\" -> eulerExplicit,\n  \
\"EulerBuiltIn\" -> eulerBuiltIn,\n  \"EulerComparison\" -> eulerComparison,\n\
  \"HiddenConstraints\" -> hiddenConstraints,\n  \"SymbolAudit\" -> \
opfSymbolsAudit,\n  \"BackgroundConditions\" -> backgroundConditions,\n  \
\"LinearizedSystem\" -> linearizedSystem,\n  \"PotentialHessianAtStationary\" \
-> potentialHessianAtStationary,\n  \"StiffnessMatrix\" -> stiffnessMatrix,\n \
 \"DispersionDeterminant\" -> dispersionDet,\n  \"ModalFrequencies\" -> \
modalFrequencies,\n  \"StableModes\" -> purelyOscillatoryModes,\n  \
\"MaintenanceSolution\" -> maintenanceSolution,\n  \"MeasurementExamples\" -> \
measurementPrototypes,\n  \"PDEBenchmark\" -> pdeBench,\n  \
\"SchrodingerBenchmark\" -> schBench,\n  \"PrecisionAudit\" -> \
precisionAudit,\n  \"StateSpaceControllability\" -> controllabilityCheck,\n  \
\"StateSpacePoles\" -> stabilityPoles\n|>;\n\n\
Export[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \"opf-rigorous-test-summary.wl\"}], \
finalSummary];\nExport[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"opf-rigorous-test-summary.mx\"}], finalSummary];\n\
Export[FileNameJoin[{NotebookDirectory[], \
\"opf-rigorous-test-summary.txt\"}], ToString[InputForm[finalSummary]], \
\"Text\"];\n\nsay[\"Rigorous OPF audit harness complete.\"];\npass[\"Summary \
artifacts exported.\"];\n```\n\n---\n\n## 26. Recommended Next Extensions\n\n\
Choose one next and extend rigorously:\n\n1. **Automated parameter sweep \
lab**\n   - random and structured parameter scans\n   - classification of \
stable/unstable branches\n\n2. **Real-data fitting harness**\n   - parameter \
estimation against observations\n   - train/test split for predictive \
validity\n\n3. **Finite-domain OPF PDE solver**\n   - explicit bounded region\
\n   - boundary conditions\n   - [NDEigensystem](paclet:ref/NDEigensystem) \
mode extraction\n\n4. **External Python/CUDA co-validation package**\n   - \
generated JSON/WXF payloads\n   - torch benchmark runner\n   - residual and \
determinant agreement tests\n\n5. **Continuous integration test pack**\n   - \
symbolic regression tests\n   - numerical benchmark thresholds\n   - \
hardware-specific performance alerts\n\n---\n\n## 27. Important Practical \
Note\n\nYour OPF instruction requests infinite working precision for all \
spatial/temporal integrations. In practical finite numerical computing, \
literal infinite precision is not available for FEM eigensolvers. The \
rigorous replacement is:\n\n- exact symbolic derivation first\n- arbitrary \
precision where supported\n- systematic convergence sweeps\n- residual-based \
acceptance\n- independent cross-validation\n\nThat is the closest \
mathematically honest implementation path for predictive testing.\n\n/exec\n\
