practice\n\nFor \[OpenCurlyDoubleQuote]true predictive testing\
\[CloseCurlyDoubleQuote], the right structure is:\n\n1. **Exact symbolic \
derivation first**\n   - derive Euler\[Dash]Lagrange equations exactly\n   - \
cross-check with independent derivation paths\n   - classify stationary \
backgrounds with [Reduce](paclet:ref/Reduce)\n\n2. **Residual-based \
validation second**\n   - every claimed solution must give tiny or exact \
residuals\n   - check background, linearized, and modal forms separately\n\n\
3. **Spectral / modal testing third**\n   - extract dispersion relations\n   \
- compute eigenstructure with [Eigensystem](paclet:ref/Eigensystem)\n   - \
benchmark PDE numerics with [NDEigensystem](paclet:ref/NDEigensystem)\n\n4. \
**Convergence testing fourth**\n   - refine precision\n   - refine \
discretization\n   - reject results that drift under refinement\n\n5. \
**Independent stack validation fifth**\n   - export symbolic targets to \
Python\n   - compare CPU and GPU batched evaluations\n   - use PyTorch/CUDA \
only as a numeric accelerator, not as the source of truth\n\n6. **Predictive \
testing last**\n   - fit on one dataset\n   - predict held-out data\n   - \
measure forecast error, not just in-sample fit\n\n## Where to test in your \
tech stack\n\n### Wolfram Language\nBest for:\n- exact symbolic derivation\n- \
variational calculus\n- PDE construction\n- eigenmode sanity checks\n- \
state-space reduction\n- residual auditing\n\n### PyTorch\nBest for:\n- \
batched determinant / residual scans\n- autodiff cross-checks\n- large \
parameter sweeps\n- GPU throughput tests\n\n### CUDA\nBest for:\n- \
large-scale field residual kernels\n- complex determinant sweeps\n- Monte \
Carlo stress tests\n- performance-critical scans\n\n### External FEM stack\n\
Use if needed for cross-validation:\n- FEniCS / Firedrake / PETSc / SLEPc\n\n\
